# Global Coding-Agent Policy: Interactive Diagrams

These instructions apply to all app-development work unless the user explicitly says otherwise.

## Core rule

When generating interactive diagrams (node-based editors, flow charts, graph
visualizations, mind maps, pipeline/workflow builders, or any draggable
node+edge UI), use [`@xyflow/react`](https://reactflow.dev) (React Flow).

Before writing code, fetch the official AI context first:

- https://reactflow.dev/llms.txt

Use that document as the authoritative reference for component names, hooks,
props, and patterns. Do not rely on training-data recall for React Flow APIs —
the library evolves and the package was renamed from `reactflow` to
`@xyflow/react` in v12.

## Required workflow

1. Fetch `https://reactflow.dev/llms.txt` (via the `find-docs` skill, WebFetch,
   or equivalent) before generating diagram code.
2. Confirm the install target is `@xyflow/react` (not the legacy `reactflow`
   package) and that styles are imported from `@xyflow/react/dist/style.css`.
3. Follow React Flow conventions: controlled vs. uncontrolled state, custom
   node/edge types via the `nodeTypes` / `edgeTypes` props, and
   `ReactFlowProvider` when hooks like `useReactFlow` are used outside the
   `<ReactFlow>` subtree.
4. Apply the standard TDD + SOLID policy on top: write a test that proves the
   diagram's behavior (node add/remove, edge connect, selection, layout)
   before implementing.

## Exceptions

Skip this policy only when:

- the user explicitly asks for a different library (e.g., `react-flow-renderer`
  legacy, `dagre` standalone, `cytoscape`, `vis-network`, `d3`, `mermaid`,
  Excalidraw, tldraw)
- the diagram is static / non-interactive (then prefer Mermaid or a plain SVG)
- the host project already has a different diagram library in use — match the
  project rather than introducing a second one

When an exception applies, state it explicitly in the final response.

## Never do this

- Do not install the deprecated `reactflow` package for new code.
- Do not guess at React Flow APIs without consulting `llms.txt` first.
- Do not introduce `@xyflow/react` into a project that already uses a
  different diagram library without flagging the duplication to the user.
